Consider constraints \(h_i(x)=0\), \(i=1,\ldots,m\), and \(g_j(x)\leq0\), \(j=1,\ldots,p\), where all functions are quadratic. Let \(I\subseteq\{1,\ldots,m\}\) index a basis of \(\{
abla h_i( ar x)\}_{i=1}^m\), and let \(A( ar x)=\{j:g_j( ar x)=0\}\). The relaxed constant-rank condition holds at \( ar x\) when, for every \(J\subseteq A( ar x)\), the rank of \(\{
abla h_i(x):i\in I\}\cup\{
abla g_j(x):j\in J\}\) is constant throughout some neighborhood of \( ar x\).

For a feasible point \( ar x\), let \(A( ar x)=\{j:g_j( ar x)=0\}\). The quadratic constraint system is degenerate at \( ar x\) if the family \(\{
abla h_i( ar x)\}_{i=1}^m\cup\{
abla g_j( ar x)\}_{j\in A( ar x)}\) is linearly dependent, equivalently if LICQ fails. It is strongly degenerate when no vector \(d\) satisfies \(
abla h_i( ar x)^Td=0\) for every \(i\) and \(
abla g_j( ar x)^Td<0\) for every \(j\in A( ar x)\), equivalently when MFCQ fails.

A feasible point \( ar x\) is a regular KKT point if multipliers \(\lambda\in\mathbb{R}^m\) and \(\mu\in\mathbb{R}_+^p\) exist such that \(
abla_xL( ar x,\lambda,\mu)=0\) and \(\mu_jg_j( ar x)=0\) for every \(j\), and the gradients \(\{
abla h_i( ar x)\}_{i=1}^m\cup\{
abla g_j( ar x):j\in A( ar x)\}\) are linearly independent. Under this regularity, the KKT multiplier pair is unique.
